1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:10,480
Slide 22 presents our second deep-dive 실전 사례: CASE STUDY 2: WEBVIEW2 MEMORY LEAK RESCUE: Saving a FinTech Trading Desk from Disaster.

2
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:19,278
Listen to this high-stakes Wall Street nightmare, students: a proprietary trading firm had 40 equity traders using 8GB workstations.

3
00:00:19,278 --> 00:00:24,560
They installed an unconstrained WebView2 desktop AI app to summarize earnings news.

4
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:30,140
And what happened on Monday morning at 9:30 AM when the market opened, James?

5
00:00:30,540 --> 00:00:36,072
The opening bell rang, and thousands of live market feeds flooded the system!

6
00:00:36,072 --> 00:00:43,330
The unconstrained WebView2 renderer began leaking memory, swelling from 1.2GB to 3.8GB in 60 seconds!

7
00:00:43,730 --> 00:00:46,785
The physical RAM was completely exhausted!

8
00:00:46,785 --> 00:00:57,770
The Bloomberg trading terminals froze for 45 seconds, traders couldn't execute stop-loss orders, and the firm lost $620,000 in delayed order execution!

9
00:00:58,170 --> 00:01:10,050
Look at our Spark Process Governor solution on the right: we locked every agent instance inside a Windows Job Object with a hard 200MB memory ceiling and replaced the heavy web view with native C++ IPC!

10
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:14,198
Memory usage dropped from 3.8GB to 85MB!

11
00:01:14,198 --> 00:01:21,320
100% terminal uptime during market open, and zero trading delays ever again!

12
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:28,853
Rigorous memory governance is not just good coding—it protects millions of dollars in enterprise value.

13
00:01:28,853 --> 00:01:32,040
Now let us enter the Omniscient Eye in Part 3!

